WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.477 --> 00:00:03.179
- On the surface the
dap does look like any,

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:07.189
everyday handshake, but
the dap is a gesture

00:00:07.189 --> 00:00:09.640
of solidarity.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.150
It's not just a handshake.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.250
I'm Lamont Hamilton, I'm a
interdisciplinary artist.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.420
I've been looking at
the handshake, the dap,

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.370
from a very specific and unique
African American experience.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.760
It's a acronym for dignity and pride.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.630
And it was coined by black soldiers

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:32.780
during the Vietnam war.

00:00:34.339 --> 00:00:36.370
You have the ’60's and all
these political movements

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.397
and there's a racial
consciousness that was heightened.

00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:40.920
- More power to the people!

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.490
- Now this war is happening and that moved

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:44.490
into this whole 'nother world.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:46.270
You take these young men and you put them

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.850
in this space away from
home, they're gonna make

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:49.990
a home with each other,

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:52.180
and solidarity became really important.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.320
So, that's where the dap was born out of,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.440
was a communication
that, I'm not above you,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.860
you're not above me, we're
side by side together.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.710
And that's the original dap.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.930
As black soldiers were coming home,

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.680
they were introducing this thing

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.629
that they did in ‘Nam to their friends.

00:01:08.629 --> 00:01:10.520
And then that moves on and on and on,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.920
and it is where it is today.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:15.710
Five On The Black Hand Side

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.160
is a photographic series

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.709
in which I've been looking at the dap.

00:01:19.709 --> 00:01:22.310
I've photographed about 120 handshakes.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.440
Historical narratives
are very important to me,

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.050
but that kind of new imagining,
or new vantage point,

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.830
is what I'm interested in.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.950
But I wanted to get at something deeper

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.130
than what the photographs
alone were doing.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.260
Dapline is a performance piece.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.560
We're really tryn' to
bring out that narrative

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.610
of black soldiers during the Vietnam war.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.870
- I was always interested
in these really genuine,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.850
but also complex expressions
within black culture.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.300
Dapline became a visible
reflection of very quiet moments

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.520
between black men.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.650
- The goal of what I'm
doing with the whole project

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.970
is to bring it back to
that very specific origin,

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.240
and give very specific and real credit

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.720
to this particular radical gesture

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.480
that has all this thing inside of it.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.370
We have to recognize the black experience

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.660
as a truly American experience.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.690
And then, what can I do to
move this narrative forward.

